akr 0.1
project raw-autotune

record raw-autotune.question.deleted-chroma-hull-clamp/1 : question {
    title "Should the measured-chromaticity hull clamp be restored in some form?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        e42d481 deleted constrain_region_chromaticity, chroma_hull, nearest_chroma_envelope and CHROMA_ENVELOPE_TOLERANCE. That code took the convex hull of the chromaticities measured on a region's boundary and, for every partially-clipped cell (grid.valid neither all-false nor all-true) whose reconstructed chromaticity fell outside the hull by more than the tolerance, pulled it back toward the nearest hull point. Nothing in the new path bounds chromaticity by measured chromaticity: component_coherence, luminance_support and luminance_envelope all scale magnitude, which preserves the R:G:B ratio by construction.

        The exposure is a cell with no trusted channel pair, where w_rg = w_bg = w_rb = 0 and all four spatial weights collapse to WEIGHT_FLOOR (1e-4). The 2x2 system is then PRIOR_WEIGHT 0.02 against 4e-4 of neighbour coupling — about 98% prior — so the cell converges back to initial_uv[i], the unconstrained affine/harmonic initializer's own hue, with nothing to catch it. This is the invented-magenta case the deleted test partial_reconstruction_cannot_invent_chroma_beyond_its_measured_boundary caught, asserting current[3][0] < 1.2 against an affine prediction of 2.4. That test was deleted with no replacement, and no surviving assertion in src/ or tests/ bounds reconstructed chromaticity by measured chromaticity.

        Open question: was removing the hull clamp the point of the joint estimator (on the theory that the joint solve cannot invent chroma), or collateral? If the former, the reasoning needs recording and the WEIGHT_FLOOR-collapse case needs a test showing the prior is safe there. If the latter, restore a bound and its regression test.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.deleted-chroma-hull-clamp/2 : question {
    title "Should the measured-chromaticity hull clamp be restored in some form?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        e42d481 deleted constrain_region_chromaticity, chroma_hull, nearest_chroma_envelope and CHROMA_ENVELOPE_TOLERANCE. That code took the convex hull of the chromaticities measured on a region's boundary and, for every partially-clipped cell (grid.valid neither all-false nor all-true) whose reconstructed chromaticity fell outside the hull by more than the tolerance, pulled it back toward the nearest hull point. Nothing in the new path bounds chromaticity by measured chromaticity: component_coherence, luminance_support and luminance_envelope all scale magnitude, which preserves the R:G:B ratio by construction.

        The exposure is a cell with no trusted channel pair, where w_rg = w_bg = w_rb = 0 and all four spatial weights collapse to WEIGHT_FLOOR (1e-4). The 2x2 system is then PRIOR_WEIGHT 0.02 against 4e-4 of neighbour coupling — about 98% prior — so the cell converges back to initial_uv[i], the unconstrained affine/harmonic initializer's own hue, with nothing to catch it. This is the invented-magenta case the deleted test partial_reconstruction_cannot_invent_chroma_beyond_its_measured_boundary caught, asserting current[3][0] < 1.2 against an affine prediction of 2.4. That test was deleted with no replacement, and no surviving assertion in src/ or tests/ bounds reconstructed chromaticity by measured chromaticity.

        Open question: was removing the hull clamp the point of the joint estimator (on the theory that the joint solve cannot invent chroma), or collateral? If the former, the reasoning needs recording and the WEIGHT_FLOOR-collapse case needs a test showing the prior is safe there. If the latter, restore a bound and its regression test.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        Restore a bound only for the evidence-free degeneracy, not for ordinary continuous-confidence cells. After the joint solve, a cell with no pairwise data authority and no spatial edge above WEIGHT_FLOOR is projected to the nearest point of the measured boundary's convex hull in log-chromaticity. Cells with either data or meaningful coupling remain unconstrained. The invented-magenta regression now pins this case.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.deleted-chroma-hull-clamp/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-dsc1289-20260823/1,
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1
    ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.fully-clipped-core-threshold/1 : question {
    title "Why did the fully-clipped-core test tighten from mean confidence 0.5 to 1.0 minus epsilon?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        fully_clipped_components' core seed test changed in e42d481 from `grid.valid[seed] != [false; 3]` (skip unless every channel is invalid, i.e. mean confidence >= VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX = 0.5) to `grid.confidence[seed].iter().any(|&c| c < 1.0 - EPSILON)` (skip unless every channel's mean confidence is >= 1.0 - 1e-8, reachable only when every sample in the quad reads >= CLIP_RAMP_HIGH = 0.985). That is roughly a 2x tightening of what counts as a blown core.

        Cores are what receive apply_luminance_domes' distance-weighted rim-and-slope extrapolation, which is what gives a large blown region a dome rather than a plateau. A core whose samples sit around 0.96 (mean confidence ~0.85) qualified before and does not now; it falls back to plain harmonic diffusion, which by the maximum principle cannot exceed its boundary values, giving a visibly flatter highlight. joint_log_chromaticity does not compensate: its trusted_denominator <= EPSILON branch falls through to harmonic_scalar, the same undomed field.

        Review measured fully_clipped_cores at 0 on the frames it tested, so practical exposure depends on corpus content and this may be latent. Open question: was the tightening deliberate — for instance to stop domes firing on soft shoulders — or an unconsidered consequence of moving from the binary `valid` topology to continuous confidence? If deliberate, record the threshold's justification; if not, the natural continuous analogue of the old test is mean confidence >= VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX, not >= 1.0 - EPSILON.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.fully-clipped-core-threshold/2 : question {
    title "Why did the fully-clipped-core test tighten from mean confidence 0.5 to 1.0 minus epsilon?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        fully_clipped_components' core seed test changed in e42d481 from `grid.valid[seed] != [false; 3]` (skip unless every channel is invalid, i.e. mean confidence >= VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX = 0.5) to `grid.confidence[seed].iter().any(|&c| c < 1.0 - EPSILON)` (skip unless every channel's mean confidence is >= 1.0 - 1e-8, reachable only when every sample in the quad reads >= CLIP_RAMP_HIGH = 0.985). That is roughly a 2x tightening of what counts as a blown core.

        Cores are what receive apply_luminance_domes' distance-weighted rim-and-slope extrapolation, which is what gives a large blown region a dome rather than a plateau. A core whose samples sit around 0.96 (mean confidence ~0.85) qualified before and does not now; it falls back to plain harmonic diffusion, which by the maximum principle cannot exceed its boundary values, giving a visibly flatter highlight. joint_log_chromaticity does not compensate: its trusted_denominator <= EPSILON branch falls through to harmonic_scalar, the same undomed field.

        Review measured fully_clipped_cores at 0 on the frames it tested, so practical exposure depends on corpus content and this may be latent. Open question: was the tightening deliberate — for instance to stop domes firing on soft shoulders — or an unconsidered consequence of moving from the binary `valid` topology to continuous confidence? If deliberate, record the threshold's justification; if not, the natural continuous analogue of the old test is mean confidence >= VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX, not >= 1.0 - EPSILON.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        Retain the 1.0-minus-EPSILON per-channel threshold deliberately. Luminance domes extrapolate peaks and therefore require a hard-clipped core with no surviving local evidence; soft shoulder samples, including mean confidence around 0.85, remain in harmonic reconstruction and must not receive an invented dome. A regression now distinguishes the soft-shoulder cell from the hard-clipped core.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.fully-clipped-core-threshold/1 ]
    supported_by [ @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1 ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.grid-valid-mean-confidence/1 : question {
    title "Was build_grid's switch from any-trusted-sample to mean-confidence intended?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        In e42d481 build_grid changed `valid[i][c]` from `trusted_count[i][c] > 0` (any sample in the quad was trusted, and `value[i][c]` was the mean of the trusted samples only) to `grid_confidence[i][c] < VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX`, where `grid_confidence` is the mean clip confidence over all samples and `value[i][c]` is now a trust-weighted mean over all samples including clipped ones. Only green has two samples per RGGB quad, so only green changes behaviour, and it changes in the direction that matters: a green quad with sample confidences [0.1, 1.0] was previously measured data valued at the clean sample, and is now unknown, to be resynthesized with grid.floor[i][1] = max(G1, G2) — the clipped sample — as its lower bound. Separately, mixing a clipped (always upward-biased) sample into `value` contaminates the regression points that fit_line and knee_reference_line treat as ground truth. grid.valid remains the measured/unknown authority in twelve downstream reads, including the unknown set in solve_region_direct and the pyramid/fit_line paths. This fires along the leading edge of every clipped region — the 1<->2 transition the same commit names as its critical sky gate — and the commit message does not mention it.

        Open question: was this semantic change deliberate? If it was, it needs a comment stating the intent and a test pinning the green-quad case; if it was collateral from introducing continuous confidence, it is a defect and the trusted-only mean should be restored for `value` even if `valid` stays mean-based.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.grid-valid-mean-confidence/2 : question {
    title "Was build_grid's switch from any-trusted-sample to mean-confidence intended?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        In e42d481 build_grid changed `valid[i][c]` from `trusted_count[i][c] > 0` (any sample in the quad was trusted, and `value[i][c]` was the mean of the trusted samples only) to `grid_confidence[i][c] < VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX`, where `grid_confidence` is the mean clip confidence over all samples and `value[i][c]` is now a trust-weighted mean over all samples including clipped ones. Only green has two samples per RGGB quad, so only green changes behaviour, and it changes in the direction that matters: a green quad with sample confidences [0.1, 1.0] was previously measured data valued at the clean sample, and is now unknown, to be resynthesized with grid.floor[i][1] = max(G1, G2) — the clipped sample — as its lower bound. Separately, mixing a clipped (always upward-biased) sample into `value` contaminates the regression points that fit_line and knee_reference_line treat as ground truth. grid.valid remains the measured/unknown authority in twelve downstream reads, including the unknown set in solve_region_direct and the pyramid/fit_line paths. This fires along the leading edge of every clipped region — the 1<->2 transition the same commit names as its critical sky gate — and the commit message does not mention it.

        Open question: was this semantic change deliberate? If it was, it needs a comment stating the intent and a test pinning the green-quad case; if it was collateral from introducing continuous confidence, it is a defect and the trusted-only mean should be restored for `value` even if `valid` stays mean-based.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        The split semantics are intentional. grid.value remains the continuous trust-weighted measurement used by the joint estimator, while grid.valid remains mean-confidence compatibility topology for the legacy pyramid and line-fit initializer. A split-green quad test pins the trust-weighted clean value, mean confidence, maximum floor, and not-wholly-measured classification.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.grid-valid-mean-confidence/1 ]
    supported_by [ @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1 ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.memory-planner-cannot-use-the-spatial-floor/1 : question {
    title "The --jobs planner still reserves worst-case harmonic memory the floor often does not spend"
    state open
    question """
        The memory planner cannot see the spatial highlight floor. Should it be able to?

        memory.rs sizes --jobs from a per-pixel reserve, and HighlightMethod::Harmonic contributes 64 bytes per pixel on top of the base. That reserve is chosen before any file is decoded, so the planner cannot know which frames will decline the solve. Peak RSS falls in practice on a clean batch — measured 2,749,016 KB to 2,701,532 KB on a 29-frame Sony sample — but the planned worker count does not change at all, so on a clean batch the program still under-parallelises against the memory it actually uses.

        Three options, none costed. Reserve the Current figure and grow the pool when a frame turns out to need the solver, which breaks the current guarantee that a plan cannot be exceeded. Probe the clipped-site fraction from headers or a decimated decode before planning, which costs a partial decode per file. Or accept the worst-case reserve as the price of the determinism and memory-safety guarantees, which is the current answer and may well be the right one.

        This is the concrete residue of the "overall memory usage needs tuning" item on the raw-autotune side: the wall-clock half was addressed by the floor, the planner half was not.
        """
}

record raw-autotune.question.prediction-band-below-clip-ramp/1 : question {
    title "Should apply_prediction touch measured data in the band (0.92, 0.9525)?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        apply_prediction's guard changed in e42d481 from `if confidence_at(...) < VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX { continue; }` — touching only sites at confidence >= 0.5, i.e. raw >= ~0.9525, and writing `prediction.max(original)` — to `if confidence == 0.0 || strength == 0.0 { continue; }`, which admits every site above CLIP_RAMP_LOW = 0.92, writing `original + strength * confidence * lift`.

        The band (0.92, 0.9525) is the new part, and it contains genuinely unclipped sensor data that was previously left byte-exact. Because prediction[cell][c] >= grid.floor[cell][c] and grid.floor is the MAX over the quad's samples, the lower of an RGGB quad's two greens receives a positive lift even when nothing about green clipped: at G_lower = 0.930 against G_upper = 0.960 the confidence smoothstep gives about 0.05, closing roughly 6% of the gap. Small per pixel, but applied across the whole bright half of a sky gradient it pulls each quad's two greens together and quantizes sub-quad green detail to quad resolution in the brightest real data. Above 0.9525 the new form is gentler than the old outright overwrite; only the new band is a regression.

        Open question: is admitting the sub-clip band the intended consequence of making the estimator continuous, or should the write remain gated at VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX while the lift stays continuous above it? Note this interacts with reconstruct_cfa's early return, which moved from `clipped_cfa_sites == 0` to `!has_reconstruction_authority` in the same commit and admits frames on the same 0.92 threshold; the two should be decided together.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.prediction-band-below-clip-ramp/2 : question {
    title "Should apply_prediction touch measured data in the band (0.92, 0.9525)?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        apply_prediction's guard changed in e42d481 from `if confidence_at(...) < VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX { continue; }` — touching only sites at confidence >= 0.5, i.e. raw >= ~0.9525, and writing `prediction.max(original)` — to `if confidence == 0.0 || strength == 0.0 { continue; }`, which admits every site above CLIP_RAMP_LOW = 0.92, writing `original + strength * confidence * lift`.

        The band (0.92, 0.9525) is the new part, and it contains genuinely unclipped sensor data that was previously left byte-exact. Because prediction[cell][c] >= grid.floor[cell][c] and grid.floor is the MAX over the quad's samples, the lower of an RGGB quad's two greens receives a positive lift even when nothing about green clipped: at G_lower = 0.930 against G_upper = 0.960 the confidence smoothstep gives about 0.05, closing roughly 6% of the gap. Small per pixel, but applied across the whole bright half of a sky gradient it pulls each quad's two greens together and quantizes sub-quad green detail to quad resolution in the brightest real data. Above 0.9525 the new form is gentler than the old outright overwrite; only the new band is a regression.

        Open question: is admitting the sub-clip band the intended consequence of making the estimator continuous, or should the write remain gated at VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX while the lift stays continuous above it? Note this interacts with reconstruct_cfa's early return, which moved from `clipped_cfa_sites == 0` to `!has_reconstruction_authority` in the same commit and admits frames on the same 0.92 threshold; the two should be decided together.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        Admitting the sub-0.5-confidence band is intentional and required by the continuous-confidence design. Application begins at nonzero smooth clip confidence and scales lift continuously; restoring VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX would reintroduce the binary discontinuity. A direct regression pins nonzero application below the old gate, while the DSC1289 comparison confirms the established default output remains unchanged.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.prediction-band-below-clip-ramp/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-dsc1289-20260823/1,
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1
    ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.purple-sky-standing-after-slice-5/1 : question {
    title "Whether the lavender-sky defect survives Slice 5 is unverified, not unresolved"
    state superseded
    question """
        Is the residual lavender/purple sky defect still present after the Slice 5 joint raw-domain log-chromaticity estimator?

        docs/PURPLE_SKY_PROBLEM_SCOPE.md was prepared on 2026-08-08 against ff74d3b2 and declared the defect unresolved. The Slice 5 estimator merged on 2026-08-23 in e42d481, and CHANGELOG.md records it as removing "the source of the visible 1<->2 clip-state discontinuity in skies", moving _DSC1289's rendered output by 0.286% RMSE.

        That is not the same claim. The document always described two populations with different causes — a broad faint lavender cast and edge-localized purple fringing — and the clip-state discontinuity is at most one of them. No visual pass over _DSC1282, _DSC1283, _DSC1288, _DSC1289 and _DSC1290 has been recorded since the merge, so the honest status is neither resolved nor unresolved but unverified, and the document has been marked accordingly rather than left asserting a stale verdict.

        Settling this needs a render of those five frames on the current default and someone looking at them, recorded as evidence. Until then nothing should cite that document as saying the defect is open, and nothing should cite Slice 5 as saying it is closed.
        """
}

record raw-autotune.question.purple-sky-standing-after-slice-5/2 : question {
    title "Lavender-sky after Slice 5 — answered: patch population survived, fixed by neutral fallback; faint cast separate"
    state resolved
    question """
        Is the residual lavender/purple sky defect still present after the Slice 5 joint raw-domain log-chromaticity estimator?

        docs/PURPLE_SKY_PROBLEM_SCOPE.md was prepared on 2026-08-08 against ff74d3b2 and declared the defect unresolved. The Slice 5 estimator merged on 2026-08-23 in e42d481, and CHANGELOG.md records it as removing "the source of the visible 1<->2 clip-state discontinuity in skies", moving _DSC1289's rendered output by 0.286% RMSE.

        That is not the same claim. The document always described two populations with different causes — a broad faint lavender cast and edge-localized purple fringing — and the clip-state discontinuity is at most one of them. Settling this needed a render of _DSC1282, _DSC1283, _DSC1288, _DSC1289 and _DSC1290 on the current default and someone looking at them, recorded as evidence.
        """
    resolution """
        Answered 2026-08-27 by rendering all five frames before/after the
        harmonic neutral-fallback fix (git c09b7f9,
        @raw-autotune.evidence.harmonic-neutral-fallback-20260827): the
        patch/fringe population WAS still present after Slice 5 — its cause
        was the joint solve's heterogeneity guard attenuating toward clipped
        chroma — and is eliminated on _DSC1282/1283/1290 (small residual
        patch remains top-right of _DSC1282). The broad faint lavender cast
        (_DSC1288/1289) is untouched by the fix (<0.1% pixels changed): it is
        a separate cause and is tracked as the remaining open item in
        @raw-autotune.observation.spatial-highlight-false-colour-on-clipped-foliage's
        follow-up note rather than by keeping this question open.
        """
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.purple-sky-standing-after-slice-5/1 ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.spatial-highlight-floor-not-swept/1 : question {
    title "The spatial highlight floor is conservative but unswept, and leans on a different statistic than it gates on"
    state open
    question """
        DEFAULT_SPATIAL_CLIPPED_FLOOR is 1e-5 and has never been swept. What is the right value, and against which statistic?

        Two gaps. First, the corpus measurement it leans on — docs/HDR_EVALUATION.md's census of all 58 files in raw/raw_3rd_batch, median 0.0003%, p90 1.9%, max 12.4%, nine frames above 1% and those the ones flagged by eye — counts pixels with two or more channels clipped, while the floor counts CFA sites at or above VALID_CONFIDENCE_MAX, which is a strictly larger population. The three-orders-of-magnitude margin is therefore indicative, not exact. Second, no sweep of the constant itself against the paired-camera corpus has been run.

        The sweep is cheap to set up because clipped_cfa_sites is already in every sidecar: a --dry-run --summary over the corpus gives the whole distribution without rendering, and the 29-frame sample already shows a wide empty band between 0 and 7.3e-6 sites-fraction, so the answer may be that any floor inside that band behaves identically and the constant does not matter much. That has not been established on the full corpus.

        Related and separate: reconstruct_cfa's pre-existing has_reconstruction_authority early return reports the requested method rather than Current, so a frame that does no spatial work by that route still routes to spatial_report_and_uncertainty downstream. Making the two consistent would move those frames' output, so it was left alone. Whether it should be changed is part of this question.
        """
}

record raw-autotune.question.status-doc-standing-verification/1 : question {
    title "What does docs/STATUS.md owe the removed HCHROMA line and the new final stage dump?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "docs/STATUS.md" ]
    question """
        CLAUDE.md states that docs/STATUS.md records what actually works per source and the standing verification steps. e42d481 removed the HCHROMA diagnostic line (transport_spatial_chromaticity is no longer on the Harmonic production path) and added a new post-sharpen `-final.png` stage dump reachable through --dump-stages, but left STATUS.md unchanged. The CHANGELOG gap from the same commit was closed in 4fcba63; this one was not.

        Open question: which of these belong in STATUS.md — specifically, does the -final.png dump become part of the standing verification steps (it is the only diagnostic checkpoint that can be byte-identical to a default CLI output, since output sharpening runs after gamut-post, and the true-default identity check is already recorded as evidence in @raw-autotune.evidence.sky-final-output-20260816), and should the removed HCHROMA line be struck from the per-source status or noted as ablation-only?

        Found by review of e42d481.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

