    ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.strength-compounding-is-unreachable/1 : question {
    title "Fractional reconstruction strength compounds across two stages and is unreachable today"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/api.rs", path "src/cli.rs", path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        For a fractional strength the two lifting stages compound multiplicatively. apply_sensor_knee mutates `samples` in place, build_grid then reads those already-modified samples, and apply_prediction multiplies by strength a second time against the pre-knee `original`. At strength 0.5 a site both stages want to raise ends up governed by max(original + 0.5 * knee_lift, original + 0.5 * confidence * pred_lift), where pred_lift was itself derived from a grid built on knee-modified samples — a strength-squared-flavoured composition with no defined meaning. No test covers the pair; prediction_application_is_continuous_in_confidence_and_strength exercises apply_prediction standalone.

        This is unreachable today: cli.rs:479 and api.rs:221 both `ensure!` that highlight_reconstruction == 1.0 for any spatial method. The consequence worth recording is that the entire continuous-strength machinery e42d481 adds is dead outside unit tests, and is a trap for whoever relaxes that guard.

        Open question: should continuous strength be defined and tested as a single composed scale (one strength applied once to the total lift), or should the dead machinery be removed and strength kept binary for spatial methods until there is a reason to expose it? Either answer is cheap now and expensive after someone relaxes the ensure!.

        Note: review also claimed color.rs:1169 forces raw_method = Current at strength 0.0; that could not be confirmed by grep and should be checked when this is resolved.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.strength-compounding-is-unreachable/2 : question {
    title "Fractional reconstruction strength compounds across two stages and is unreachable today"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/api.rs", path "src/cli.rs", path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        For a fractional strength the two lifting stages compound multiplicatively. apply_sensor_knee mutates `samples` in place, build_grid then reads those already-modified samples, and apply_prediction multiplies by strength a second time against the pre-knee `original`. At strength 0.5 a site both stages want to raise ends up governed by max(original + 0.5 * knee_lift, original + 0.5 * confidence * pred_lift), where pred_lift was itself derived from a grid built on knee-modified samples — a strength-squared-flavoured composition with no defined meaning. No test covers the pair; prediction_application_is_continuous_in_confidence_and_strength exercises apply_prediction standalone.

        This is unreachable today: cli.rs:479 and api.rs:221 both `ensure!` that highlight_reconstruction == 1.0 for any spatial method. The consequence worth recording is that the entire continuous-strength machinery e42d481 adds is dead outside unit tests, and is a trap for whoever relaxes that guard.

        Open question: should continuous strength be defined and tested as a single composed scale (one strength applied once to the total lift), or should the dead machinery be removed and strength kept binary for spatial methods until there is a reason to expose it? Either answer is cheap now and expensive after someone relaxes the ensure!.

        Note: review also claimed color.rs:1169 forces raw_method = Current at strength 0.0; that could not be confirmed by grep and should be checked when this is resolved.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        Define fractional strength as one blend of the complete full-strength target: build the sensor-knee and spatial result at full authority, then compute source + strength * (target - source) once. A regression proves exact half-strength composition. CLI and API guards remain full-strength-only for spatial methods, and inspection confirmed the zero-strength routing is not forced to Current in color.rs.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.strength-compounding-is-unreachable/1 ]
    supported_by [
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1,
        @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-full-suite-20260823/1
    ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.unfitted-partial-cell-floor/1 : question {
    title "What replaces keep_unfitted_partial_cells' hard floor when every colour-line fit is rejected?"
    state superseded
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        e42d481 deleted keep_unfitted_partial_cells from both harmonic_prediction call sites. It pinned a partially-clipped channel to grid.floor[i][target] whenever best_fits returned None for that channel, and its own comment gave the reasoning: a partially clipped coloured highlight still has a measured hue, and without a trusted line its saturated sample is the only defensible lower bound, because a spatial guess would import a neighbour's colour.

        The replacement, continuous_luminance_support, is an ungated weighted r^2: unlike fit_line it has no acceptance threshold at all, it simply returns a value in [0, 1], and luminance_support[i][target] is additionally floored at the product of the cell's confidences. So for a cell where every colour-line fit was rejected, the neighbour-imported hue that was previously overwritten outright now survives at component_coherence * luminance_support of its magnitude rather than at zero. Concretely, a saturated red object beside a clipped sky can now tint the sky's missing channel a little where before it could not tint it at all.

        Open question: is a continuous attenuation the intended replacement for the hard floor, and if so what bounds it when no fit is trustworthy? Either continuous_luminance_support needs an acceptance threshold matching fit_line's, or the grid.floor pin needs restoring for the no-fit case.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
}

record raw-autotune.question.unfitted-partial-cell-floor/2 : question {
    title "What replaces keep_unfitted_partial_cells' hard floor when every colour-line fit is rejected?"
    state resolved
    scope [ path "src/raw_highlight.rs" ]
    question """
        e42d481 deleted keep_unfitted_partial_cells from both harmonic_prediction call sites. It pinned a partially-clipped channel to grid.floor[i][target] whenever best_fits returned None for that channel, and its own comment gave the reasoning: a partially clipped coloured highlight still has a measured hue, and without a trusted line its saturated sample is the only defensible lower bound, because a spatial guess would import a neighbour's colour.

        The replacement, continuous_luminance_support, is an ungated weighted r^2: unlike fit_line it has no acceptance threshold at all, it simply returns a value in [0, 1], and luminance_support[i][target] is additionally floored at the product of the cell's confidences. So for a cell where every colour-line fit was rejected, the neighbour-imported hue that was previously overwritten outright now survives at component_coherence * luminance_support of its magnitude rather than at zero. Concretely, a saturated red object beside a clipped sky can now tint the sky's missing channel a little where before it could not tint it at all.

        Open question: is a continuous attenuation the intended replacement for the hard floor, and if so what bounds it when no fit is trustworthy? Either continuous_luminance_support needs an acceptance threshold matching fit_line's, or the grid.floor pin needs restoring for the no-fit case.

        Found by review of e42d481; not fixed in 4fcba63.
        """
    resolution """
        Keep continuous attenuation only when the target channel has a colour-line fit accepted by the existing sample, conditioning, and R² gates. If every fit is rejected, neighbour-imported luminance support is zero and recomposition leaves a partial cell at its sensor floor. The smooth neutral fallback is reserved for cells hard-clipped in every channel. A regression exposes high ungated support and verifies it becomes zero without an accepted fit.
        """
    derived_from [ @raw-autotune.requirement.slice-5-continuous-log-chromaticity ]
    supersedes [ @raw-autotune.question.unfitted-partial-cell-floor/1 ]
    supported_by [ @raw-autotune.evidence.slice5-question-focused-regressions-20260823/1 ]
}
